libFuzzer targets for the parsers of CK Core CDE
================================================

This directory holds fuzz targets for parsers that read data an attacker or a
damaged file can control: the dtspcd request layer, the dtlogin XDMCP request
handlers, and the session, palette and window property readers of dtsession
and dtwm.  No production source is changed.

How a target works
------------------

The host is macOS without Motif and without the daemons' environment.  So, like
the regression tests under programs/*/tests, each target reads the production
source text, cuts the parser functions out of it, and compiles them against
small stand-ins for everything around them (sockets, widgets, Xlib replies,
the display list).  run-fuzz.py does the extraction and writes the result to
DIR/build/NAME/types.h and production.h.  The harness fuzz-NAME.c includes both.
The top comment of every harness says what is stubbed and how the input is laid
out.  If a function is renamed or moved in the production source, the extraction
stops with a message that names the function.

Requirements on the host:

  - the Homebrew clang, /opt/homebrew/opt/llvm/bin/clang (environment
    variable CLANG overrides the path); it must accept -fsanitize=fuzzer
  - the XQuartz headers in /opt/X11/include (XINCLUDE overrides), used by
    dtsession-palette, dtwm-property and dtlogin-xdmcp
  - for dtlogin-xdmcp also libXdmcp and libXau in /opt/X11/lib (XLIB overrides)

Running
-------

  python3 cde/fuzz/run-fuzz.py --list

  python3 cde/fuzz/run-fuzz.py --target spc-proto --seconds 60 \
      --scratch /Volumes/S8T/tmp/ck-core-cde/phase1-20261112/tests-scratch/fuzz

  for t in $(python3 cde/fuzz/run-fuzz.py --list | cut -d' ' -f1); do
      python3 cde/fuzz/run-fuzz.py --target $t --seconds 60 --scratch DIR
  done

  --worktree DIR   read the production sources from another checkout
                   (default: the checkout that holds this script)
  --recover-ub     report undefined behaviour and go on, listing the first
                   report of each location at the end; address errors, timeouts
                   and out-of-memory inputs still stop the run
  --work-limit N   dtwm-session only: drop an input after N fscanf calls, so
                   that the run can go on after the unbounded record counts
                   of that reader have been reported

The build uses -fsanitize=fuzzer,address,undefined -fno-sanitize-recover=undefined
-g -O1 -std=gnu23.  libFuzzer runs with -max_len=4096 -rss_limit_mb=2048
-timeout=5 and one process, so it uses one CPU.

Output below DIR
----------------

  build/NAME/        generated headers and the fuzzer binary
  corpus/NAME/       the corpus as it grew; the seeds in cde/fuzz/corpus/NAME
                     are read but never written
  crashes/NAME/      crash-*, timeout-* and oom-* inputs as libFuzzer wrote
                     them, and min-* the minimized versions
  logs/NAME.log      the complete libFuzzer output with the sanitizer reports
  tmp/               TMPDIR of the run

The exit status is 1 when a crash, a timeout or an out-of-memory input was
found.  To look at a finding again:

  DIR/build/NAME/fuzz-NAME DIR/crashes/NAME/min-crash-HASH

Seeds
-----

cde/fuzz/corpus/NAME/ holds two to five small valid inputs per target in the
layout of the harness.  make-seeds.py writes them again.

Targets
-------

  spc-proto          dtspcd request wire layer (DtEncap/spc-proto.c): header
                     decode, request read with its length limit, multi-packet
                     string lists, the counted-string decoders for application,
                     device, logfile and register requests, the termios request
  dtlogin-xdmcp      dtlogin XDMCP request handlers (xdmcp.c): query, indirect
                     query, forward query, request, manage and keep-alive, with
                     policy.c, protodpy.c, xdmauth.c, auth.c and netaddr.c
                     compiled in; packets are decoded by libXdmcp
  dtwm-session       dtwm front panel session file (Session.c:
                     SessionRestoreData)
  dtsession-client   dtsession CDE 1.0 client file (SmRestore.c:
                     ParseClientFile, the quoting parser GetSmartString)
  dtsession-palette  dtsession color server palette file and palette directory
                     list (SrvFile_io.c)
  dtwm-property      dtwm window property readers (WmProperty.c): normal hints,
                     WM_STATE, motif hints and info, UTF8 strings, colormap
                     windows, WM_PROTOCOLS and _MWM_MESSAGES

What a finding means
--------------------

A stand-in that is too loose reports errors that cannot happen in the real
program.  Every report must be read against the production code at the lines
the stack names before it is called a bug.  The harnesses keep the stand-ins to
the promises of the real interface (for example the Xlib stand-in never returns
more items than were asked for).
